<section class="page-head">
  <p class="kicker">Limb IX</p>
  <h1>Wizardry &amp; Rituals</h1>
  <p class="lede muted">A wizard is not someone who breaks the laws of nature. A wizard is someone who has
  studied long enough to act with precision where others act at random — and ritual is the technology by
  which a decision is made to hold.</p>
</section>

<!-- ============================== THE PREMISE ============================= -->
<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Begin Here</p>
    <h2>What a Wizard Actually Is</h2>
    <p class="lede">The word carries two thousand years of borrowed glamour, and underneath it is something
    plainer and more demanding.</p>

    <p><em>Wizard</em> comes from Middle English <em>wysard</em> — simply "wise one", from <em>wys</em>, with
    the same root as <em>wit</em>. ◆ Scholarship. It did not originally mean a worker of miracles. It meant
    someone who had accumulated knowledge to the point where their judgement was worth seeking, and who was
    therefore slightly outside the ordinary social order. The druid, the cunning man, the hedge-witch and the
    village herbalist were all, functionally, this.</p>

    <p>This order holds to that reading, which has three consequences. A wizard studies — the craft is a
    curriculum, not a gift. A wizard works with the actual grain of things rather than against it. And a
    wizard's power is entirely ordinary power: knowledge, attention, timing, reputation and the willingness
    to act when others hesitate. ◆ We claim nothing beyond that, and we think nothing beyond that is needed
    to justify the word.</p>

    <p>Ritual is the other half. Not a way of petitioning the universe, but a way of operating on yourself and
    on a group with far more reliability than intention alone has ever managed.</p>

    <p class="muted small">Grading throughout: <span class="sci">◆ established science</span> ·
    <span class="hist">◆ scholarship and history</span> · <span class="spec">◆ speculative and contested</span> ·
    <span class="myth">◆ myth, tradition and primary esoteric text</span>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

