    <p class="plate-note">All four are supported by actual research rather than assertion. ◆ Synchrony
    studies find that moving or chanting in time raises subsequent cooperation and perceived closeness,
    and that <em>costly</em> or effortful rituals bind harder. ◆ Anxiety work finds that performing a
    prescribed sequence before a stressful task lowers measured arousal and improves performance — and that
    this holds even when the sequence is invented by the experimenter and has no cultural content
    whatsoever. That last finding is the most important one on this page, because it tells you the power is
    in the <em>form</em>, not in the correspondence tables.</p>
