<!-- ==================== PLATE V — BUILD YOUR OWN ========================= -->
<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Practice</p>
    <h2>Build One Yourself</h2>
    <p class="lede">Borrowed ceremony usually feels hollow because it answers someone else's question. Yours
    will be clumsier and will work better. Six components, and the order would say all six are necessary.</p>

    <details class="scroll" open>
      <summary>1 · One sentence of purpose</summary>
      <p>Write what this rite is for in a single sentence, in plain language, with no symbolism in it at all.
      If you cannot, you are not ready to perform it — you are still deciding. "I am ending my working
      relationship with X and I want to stop rehearsing the argument." That is a purpose. "Transformation" is
      not.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="scroll">
      <summary>2 · A boundary in time and space</summary>
      <p>A definite start and a definite end, in a place you then leave. This is the single component that
      converts an activity into a ritual, and it is the one most often skipped. Light something at the
      beginning and extinguish it at the end if you want the simplest possible version.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="scroll">
      <summary>3 · Something irreversible and physical</summary>
      <p>Burn, bury, cut, pour, plant, carve, throw into water. Not visualise — <em>do</em>. The nervous
      system treats an unrecoverable act very differently from an imagined one, and this is the hinge the
      whole structure turns on. If nothing in your rite could go wrong, nothing in it is real.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="scroll">
      <summary>4 · A witness, where the matter concerns others</summary>
      <p>Publicly witnessed commitment holds dramatically better than private resolution. This is why
      weddings have guests and why anonymous promises evaporate. One person who will ask you about it in a
      month is worth more than any amount of ceremony performed alone.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="scroll">
      <summary>5 · Repetition, or a date</summary>
      <p>Either the rite recurs — same hour, same place, seasonally — or it is pinned to a specific date and
      kept. Solstices and new moons work not because of the sky but because they are unarguable coordinates
      that you did not choose for convenience.</p>
    </details>

    <details class="scroll">
      <summary>6 · A closing, and then food</summary>
      <p>State plainly that it is finished, undo whatever you did to open, and then eat something with other
      people if you can. Reincorporation is a real stage and skipping it is how people end up unmoored for
      days. The meal afterwards is not an afterthought; in most traditions on record it is half the rite.</p>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>

