<!-- =========================== THE LEDGER ================================ -->
<section class="wrap">
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h2>What Holds Up</h2>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Ritualised sequences reduce anxiety before stressful performance, including invented ones with no tradition behind them.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Behavioural synchrony — moving, singing or chanting in time — increases cooperation, trust and perceived similarity.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Costly and effortful rites bind groups harder, which is a measured result and an uncomfortable one.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Rhythm entrains neural oscillation; restricted sensory input shifts state reliably.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Publicly witnessed commitment outperforms private intention by a wide margin.</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> The separation–liminality–reincorporation structure recurs across unrelated cultures.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> Expectancy and setting shape outcome powerfully — the ritual surrounding a treatment is part of the treatment.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h2>What Does Not</h2>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><span class="myth">◆</span> <strong>That a circle keeps anything out.</strong> It marks; it does not seal. Treating it as armour builds dependence and, in some people, genuine fear.</li>
        <li><span class="myth">◆</span> <strong>That correspondence tables are discovered rather than assigned.</strong> They differ between traditions, which is fatal to the claim.</li>
        <li><span class="spec">◆</span> <strong>That group ritual produces effects at a distance.</strong> The large-scale meditation and prayer studies have not survived proper controls.</li>
        <li><span class="myth">◆</span> <strong>That ancient wording is more potent.</strong> Barbarous names work by sounding strange, which is a real psychological effect and not a linguistic one.</li>
        <li><span class="myth">◆</span> <strong>That ritual failure indicates spiritual interference.</strong> It usually indicates an unclear purpose, which is stage one.</li>
        <li><span class="spec">◆</span> <strong>That any of this requires a lineage.</strong> The measured effects come from the form.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<!-- ============================== THE DANGERS ============================ -->
<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame caution">
    <p class="kicker">Not Romanticised</p>
    <h2>The Actual Dangers of This Craft</h2>
    <p>They are psychological and social, and this order takes them far more seriously than any question
    about entities, because they are what damages people year after year.</p>
    <ul class="biblio">
      <li><strong>Grandiosity.</strong> The characteristic occupational hazard. Signs: a growing conviction of being chosen, irritation at being questioned, and an expanding sense that ordinary rules are for other people.</li>
      <li><strong>Destabilisation.</strong> Intensive practice can precipitate genuine crisis, particularly with any psychiatric history. If sleep breaks down or the boundaries of ordinary reality do not re-establish, that is a clinician's matter, now.</li>
      <li><strong>Compulsion.</strong> A ritual you cannot omit without dread has become an anxiety disorder wearing robes. Protection that increases fear is not protection.</li>
      <li><strong>Avoidance.</strong> Working a spell on a problem you could simply address is the most common failure mode in modern practice, and the least discussed.</li>
      <li><strong>Exploitation.</strong> Groups with secrecy, hierarchy and a charismatic teacher are structurally the same shape as the ones that go wrong. See <a href="/occult/#black-tribunal">the Black Tribunal, Limb VIII</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="muted small">The order's rule: any practice that makes you harder to be honest with has failed,
    whatever it produced.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<!-- ============================== HISTORY ================================ -->
<section class="wrap">
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h2>Where the Modern Craft Comes From</h2>
      <ul class="timeline">
        <li><span class="when">c. 300 CE</span> The Greek Magical Papyri — the real working manuals of late antiquity, full of recipes, names and practical instruction.</li>
        <li><span class="when">c. 1450</span> The Key of Solomon circulates; grimoire magic is a learned, literate, overwhelmingly Christian pursuit.</li>
        <li><span class="when">1584</span> Reginald Scot's <em>Discoverie of Witchcraft</em> exposes conjuring tricks to argue that accused witches are innocent — the first sceptical classic, written to save lives.</li>
        <li><span class="when">1801</span> Francis Barrett's <em>The Magus</em> revives ceremonial magic for a new century, largely by copying Agrippa.</li>
        <li><span class="when">1855</span> Éliphas Lévi fixes the modern image — the pentagram, the correspondences, the tarot link — much of it his own synthesis.</li>
        <li><span class="when">1888</span> The Golden Dawn is founded on a forged credential and produces the system nearly everything since descends from.</li>
        <li><span class="when">1904–47</span> Crowley's Thelema; the Book of the Law, the grade structure, and a great deal of deliberate provocation.</li>
        <li><span class="when">1920s</span> Austin Osman Spare develops sigils and the technique of deliberate forgetting.</li>
        <li><span class="when">1954</span> Gardner publishes; Wicca enters the world as a religion seventy years old.</li>
        <li><span class="when">1970s</span> Chaos magic strips the lineage and keeps the techniques, treating belief itself as a tool.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="frame reveal">
      <h2>Reading</h2>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> <strong>Ronald Hutton</strong>, <em>The Triumph of the Moon</em> (1999) — the honest history of modern pagan witchcraft, by a historian who likes his subject.</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> <strong>Owen Davies</strong>, <em>Grimoires: A History of Magic Books</em> (2009) — where the texts actually came from.</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> <strong>Hans Dieter Betz (ed.)</strong>, <em>The Greek Magical Papyri in Translation</em> — the primary sources, startling and often very practical.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> <strong>Harvey Whitehouse</strong>, <em>Inheritance</em> (2024) and his modes-of-religiosity work — ritual, frequency and group cohesion, measured.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">◆</span> <strong>Dimitris Xygalatas</strong>, <em>Ritual: How Seemingly Senseless Acts Make Life Worth Living</em> (2022) — the field science, including firewalking with heart monitors.</li>
        <li><span class="hist">◆</span> <strong>Victor Turner</strong>, <em>The Ritual Process</em> (1969) — liminality and communitas, still the key text.</li>
        <li><span class="myth">◆</span> <strong>Israel Regardie</strong>, <em>The Golden Dawn</em> — the system itself, in full, as primary material.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦ ✦ ✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--creed">
    <div class="creed-glyph">{% glyph "star" %}</div>
    <p class="kicker" style="text-align:center">The Wizard's Rule</p>
    <p class="creed-line">Know, Dare, Will, Keep Silent.</p>
    <p class="muted small" style="text-align:center;max-width:56ch;margin:0 auto">
      Lévi's four powers of the sphinx, and this order reads them without mystification. <em>Know</em>: study
      first, always, and in the actual sources. <em>Dare</em>: act, rather than endlessly preparing to act.
      <em>Will</em>: want one thing clearly enough to decline the others. <em>Keep silent</em>: not because
      secrecy is sacred, but because announcing a resolution gives you the satisfaction of having kept it and
      is the most reliable way to never do the thing at all. ◆ That last one, unexpectedly, has experimental
      support.</p>
